   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 8.1. Increased capacity of member States to develop new or reformed sustainable national social protection strategies, policies or legal frameworks to extend coverage and enhance benefit adequacy</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Following ratification of the Social Security (Minimum Standards Convention), 1952 No102 by the Russian Federation and its entry into force, the Government of Russia, on 1 April 2020, approved the indexation of social pensions which contribute to an increase in benefits. In line with its international commitments, Government also (i) approved in 2021 the indexation of the old-age (insurance based) pensions which contributed to a 6.6 % increase in  the  pension amount; ii) further operationalized the Strategy for the Pension System Development till 2030 reflecting the methodology for identification of a standard beneficiary for pensions benefits under C 102. Government proceeded with: i) further alignment of the national pension system with the provisions of the Convention and ii) the preparation of the first national report on the implementation of  Convention No. 102 to be submitted to the ILO Committee of Experts on the Application of Conventions (CEACR). The Russian Tripartite Commission meets regularly to discuss improvement of social protection system and arrives to decisions through social dialogue. The first report for submission to ILO CEACR was drafted with social partners participating in the consultations. Following close collaboration between the ILO, the Ministry of Labour and Social Protection, Pension Fund and social partners in the process of the ratification of the Social Security (Minimum Standards) Convention, 1952, No 102, technical assistance in this period focused on priority areas for the implementation. These included: i) organization of three tripartite consultation on pension system improvements and the role of social dialogue (organized by the Officein 2020 and 2021); ii) participation of the constituents in the Global round table on pension systems developments organized by the Office in December 2020; iii) three technical notes reviewing the methodology for the determination of standard beneficiary and pension replacement rates; iv) consultations on the content and process of drafting of the first national report on the implementation of C 102 with good practice examples.

Given the complexity and the significance of the pension system reform in the Russian Federation (RF), a case study documenting the pension system in RF was drafted as a contribution to ILO&apos;s global compendium of case studies documenting pension systems, and reversal of privatization. Furthermore, briefs with options to introduce mandatory unemployment insurance were drafted and experience about the introduction of so called &#x2018;social contract&apos; which aims to support pathways for social assistance linked to active labour market policies was documented.</narrative>
   </description>
